KG42.register("rules-9177fcdc5ab62ac330cd44b0",{"id":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading::v141-c-R017","paper":"12-p2p_microgrid_trading","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R017","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03","name":"Prosumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Prosumer participation is modeled as competitive, without aggregation with energy consumers and other prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. Prosumers with flexible demand are included as players in the bidding game. Scope: Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The prosumer has flexible demand.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.prosumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.prosumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation. | Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-36"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R017-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Prosumer participation is modeled as competitive, without aggregation with energy consumers and other prosumers to achieve higher benefits through cooperation. Scope: Participation assumptions of the Elecbay P2P bidding simulation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-30","v141-b-g01-ev-34","v141-b-g01-ev-35"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R017-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Prosumers with flexible demand are included as players in the bidding game. Scope: Players in the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game. Condition: The prosumer has flexible demand.","expression":"\\forall i \\in \\{\\text{prosumers with flexible demand}\\}: i \\in N","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36","v141-b-g01-ev-37","v141-b-g01-ev-32","v141-b-g01-ev-33"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R017-C003","kind":"logical","role":"supporting","statement":"The player set of the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game is N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game and N is the set of players in the game. Scope: The single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game.","expression":"N = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, i, \\ldots, n\\}","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["participation.prosumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-ev-36"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-30","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4. Simulation of P2P bidding","anchor_text":"means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with is each other for achieving higher bene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"1. The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation. 2. Energy consumers and prosumers are considered as “good citizens”, who contribute to maintaining local energy balance. 3. Energy suppliers (i.e. the electricity retailers) are passive peers in this paper. They buy energy from energy prosumers with low unit prices, and sell energy to energy consumers and prosumers with high unit prices. Energy consumers and prosumers consider energy suppliers as the last peer to trade with during the P2P bidding. This assumption is consistent with the reality in many countries such as the UK. 4. Service charges of Elecbay are neglected in this paper"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game. Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. Denote N = {1, 2, …, i …, n}, where n is the number of players in the game, and N is the set of players in the game."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-32","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M03-participation","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1. Roles of flexible demand and storage in Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"Applied Energy 220 (2018) 1–12 m and prosumers in P2P energy trading mainly rely on the scheduling o","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"DGs from intermittent renewable energy are able to be managed through disconnecting/reconnecting operations or derating maximum power outputs for energy trading purposes. However, those techniques are not investigated in this paper because it will be more beneficial for each individual peer to always keep its DG connected and generating maximum power, considering the zero marginal cost of renewable energy. Although energy storage systems are able to provide significant amount of flexibility for P2P energy trading, they are still generally very expensive. Therefore, this paper focuses on investigating the feasibility of P2P energy trading with flexible demand scheduling only. Specifically, electric water heaters with tanks are considered as representatives. The use of energy storage was not considered in this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-33","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r2__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.1. Roles of flexible demand and storage in Peer-to-Peer energy trading","anchor_text":"ng p p g g y gy g with ﬂexible demand scheduling only. Speciﬁcally, electric water hea- ters with ta","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"Note that in this paper, the flexible demand is scheduled without sacrificing any satisfaction of end users. The flexible demands considered in the case study of this paper are electric water heaters with tanks, which have thermal storage capability. This capability results in the fact that there are many different heating schedules that are all able to totally satisfy users’ demand (in this case, to prepare enough hot water with required temperature for users throughout the day), and thus brings flexibility for scheduling. For example, to prepare required amount of hot water with required temperature to be used at 5:00 p.m., the water can be pre-heated either during 3:00–4:00 p.m. or during 4:00–5:00 p.m. without any difference in terms of satisfying users’ hot water need. In other words, by limiting the minimum and maximum water temperature in tank as presented in Section 4.2.1, the scheduling of water heaters will not cause any dissatisfaction for end users. This way of dealing with flexible demands is commonly used in many existing studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise satisfaction of end users will bring more flexibility for scheduling but involve more social cost at the same time, which is not considered in this paper."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-34","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r3__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2. Simulation of a single time period using game theory","anchor_text":"th isting studies such as [24,25]. Beyond this way, to compromise sa tisfaction of end users will br","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"There are two basic types of games in game theory: cooperative games and non-cooperative games. A cooperative game is a game where groups of players enforce cooperative behaviour, and hence the game is a competition between coalitions of players, rather than between individual players. A non-cooperative game is a game in which players make decisions independently, for example, auctions and strategic voting [26]. A Nash equilibrium is a solution of a non-cooperative game involving two or more players, in which each player is assumed to know the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has anything to gain by changing only their own strategy [27]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-35","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2. Simulation of a single time period using game theory","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"During the P2P bidding for a single energy exchange time period, the interaction among peers is modelled as a non-cooperative game, due to the assumptions made at the beginning of this section. The solution of the game, in which no peer is able to increase the benefit by modifying only its own placed orders, is the Nash equilibrium, which is considered as the result of P2P bidding through Elecbay."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-37","location":{"location_id":"src-c1934be9b4-p05-c2r4__v141-a-M03-bidding","page":5,"printed_page":null,"section":"4.2.1. The formulation of P2P bidding as a non-cooperative game","anchor_text":"ed the equilibrium strategies of the other players, and no player has any es thing to gain by changi","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Strategies of a player are the decided status of flexible demand, which directly affect the amount of electricity to be injected to (positive value) or absorbed from (negative value) the Microgrid and are denoted by s_i^{j_i}, j_i = 1,2,… ,m_i, where m_i stands for the number of strategies that …"}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"The rule concerns participation/admission of prosumers; it selects no design alternative or operational decision. The source symbols i, N and n in the supporting evidence are an index, a player set and a player count, not decision variables.","empty_theta_reason":"No fixed parameter is defined in this participation rule. n is the number of players in the game, not a fixed input parameter to this rule; N is a set. Neither is represented as a theta object."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M03-bidding","v141-a-M03-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.prosumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-30","quote":"The P2P energy trading through “Elecbay” is competitive, which means energy consumers and prosumers are not aggregated with each other for achieving higher benefits via co-operation."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-ev-36","quote":"Players of the game are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"4e14e2cfc024a4554b14852a319e063bf2333664dad9f3c81aa148c7e5fd3595","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The rule has a source-supported mathematical component: the single-period non-cooperative P2P bidding game defines player set N = {1, 2, …, i …, n} and states that players are energy consumers and prosumers with flexible demand. C002 formalizes prosumer inclusion as membership in N, and C003 transcribes the player-set definition. The competitive no-aggregation assumption in C001 remains procedural and is not formalized. No x/theta are declared because the source objects involved are an index, a player set and a player count, not design/operational decision variables or fixed parameters.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R017-C002","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 formalizes prosumer participation as membership i ∈ N in the player set of the single-period non-cooperative bidding game. It is bound to the frozen node participation.prosumer. The frozen rule has no edges, so edge_ids is empty; no graph relationship is invented."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R017-C003","node_ids":["participation.prosumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 transcribes the source definition of the player set N = {1, ..., n} that contains the prosumer players of the single-period non-cooperative bidding game. It binds to participation.prosumer; the frozen rule has no edges, so edge_ids is empty."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
